  m_Name: LoadTransfer
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  lessonId: civil-craft-load-transfer-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Load Transfer
  lessonImage: {fileID: 0}
  popupDescription: "<b><#E09500>Load Transfer</color></b> is the process through which forces from an applied load travel through connected structural components toward supports and foundations.\n\nKey points:\n- Vehicle weight creates a load on the bridge deck.\n- Connected components transfer forces through the bridge.\n- Cables can carry tension; towers primarily carry compression.\n- Supports and foundations carry forces into the ground.\n\nSuspension example:\nDeck → Hangers → Main Cables → Towers and Anchorages → Ground\n\nThis lesson has been added to your <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "<b><#E09500>Load Transfer</color></b> describes how forces travel through connected structural components until they reach the supports, foundations, and eventually the ground. It focuses on the <b><#E09500>load path</color></b>; load distribution describes how that load is shared among members and supports.\n\nA vehicle applies its weight to the deck. The deck and its connected components must provide a continuous path that carries those forces toward suitable supports. The path depends on the type of bridge and how its parts are connected.\n\n<b><#E09500>Example: a suspension bridge</color></b>\nDeck → Hangers → Main Cables → Towers and Anchorages → Ground\n\nIn this example, hangers carry load from the deck to the main cables. The cables carry tension, pass over towers, and are secured at anchorages. Towers carry compressive forces toward the ground, while anchor points resist the cables' pull. This is one possible structural arrangement, not a requirement to use the same design on every crossing.\n\nFor <b><#E09500>Silas's eastern passage</color></b>, rope and the existing wooden towers offer another way to support connected bridge components. Think carefully about their connections and the available anchor points. If you use those supports, make sure they form part of a continuous load path. Every component has a role in carrying and transferring the vehicle's load safely."
